Implementation Manual

1. Program Overview
The Homework Tracker was developed using Java and JavaFX. JavaFX is used to create the graphical user interface,
while Java classes and methods handle assignment information and program functionality.
The application uses object-oriented programming, including classes, inheritance, encapsulation, and polymorphism.

2. Class Structure
The program contains the following main classes:

Assignment.java:
The Assignment class is the parent class. It stores the information shared by all assignments.

Its fields include:
assignmentName
description
dueDate
priority
assignmentType
completed

The class includes getter and setter methods, as well as methods to mark an assignment complete and determine whether an assignment is overdue.

Homework.java:
Represents homework assignments and inherits the shared fields and methods from Assignment.

Project.java:
Represents project assignments and inherits from Assignment.

Exam.java:
Represents exam assignments and inherits from Assignment.

Essay.java:
Represents essay assignments and inherits from Assignment.

Quiz.java:
Represents quiz assignments and inherits from Assignment.

Each subclass uses a constructor that calls the parent class constructor with its corresponding assignment type.

HomeworkTracker.java:
This is the main application class. It creates the JavaFX interface and manages the assignment list, user input, filtering, sorting, and button actions.

3. Data Storage
The program stores assignments using an ArrayList:

private List<Assignment> assignments = new ArrayList<>();

The ArrayList allows the application to store multiple assignment objects. Each object can represent a different assignment type.
The program stores assignments in memory while it is running. The current implementation does not save assignments permanently after the application closes.

4. Graphical User Interface
The interface is created in the start(Stage stage) method of HomeworkTracker.java.
The program uses the following JavaFX components:
GridPane: Organizes the input fields, filters, buttons, and assignment display into rows and columns.
FlowPane: Displays assignment cards and allows them to wrap onto additional rows.
HBox: Places related buttons and options next to one another.
VBox: Organizes the main application layout.
TextField: Allows users to enter assignment names and descriptions.
DatePicker: Allows users to select assignment due dates.
RadioButton: Allows users to select priority, assignment type, filters, and sorting options.
Label: Displays instructions, messages, and assignment information.
Button: Allows users to perform application actions.

5. Main Methods and Their Functions
start(Stage stage)
Creates the application window, initializes the interface components, organizes the layout, and connects buttons to their corresponding methods.

createAssignment()
Collects the information entered by the user and creates the correct assignment object based on the selected assignment type. 
It also checks that the assignment name and due date have been entered.

addAssignment()
Creates a new assignment and adds it to the ArrayList. It then refreshes the assignment display.

findAssignment(String name)
Searches the ArrayList for an assignment with a matching name. The method uses equalsIgnoreCase() so that capitalization does not affect the search.

editAssignment()
Finds an existing assignment, creates an updated assignment object, and replaces the original object in the ArrayList.

deleteAssignment()
Finds the selected assignment and removes it from the ArrayList.

markAssignmentComplete()
Finds the selected assignment and calls its markComplete() method.

isOverdue(Assignment assignment)
Checks whether an assignment is overdue by using the isOverdue() method from the Assignment class.

applyFilterAndSort()
Applies the selected priority, assignment type, and status filters. It then sorts the remaining assignments according to the selected sorting option.

priorityRank(String priority)
Assigns a numerical ranking to each priority level so assignments can be sorted from High to Low.

displayAllAssignments()
Displays every assignment in the ArrayList without applying the selected filters.

displayAssignments(List<Assignment> filteredAssignments)
Clears the previous assignment display and creates a label for each assignment. Each label displays the assignment name, description, due date, priority,
type, and status.

clearAssignmentFields()
Clears the assignment name, description, and due date fields and restores the default priority and assignment type.

clearForm()
Clears the input fields, removes all assignments from the ArrayList, resets the filters and sorting options, and clears the assignment display.

6. Filtering and Sorting Implementation
The filtering process begins by creating a copy of the assignment ArrayList. 
The program uses removeIf() to remove assignments that do not match the selected filters.
The sorting process uses Java's sort() method and lambda expressions.
Name sorting uses compareToIgnoreCase().
Due date sorting uses compareTo().
Priority sorting uses the numerical values returned by priorityRank().
After filtering and sorting, the program calls displayAssignments() to update the interface.

7. Object-Oriented Programming Concepts
The project demonstrates several object-oriented programming concepts.
Inheritance: Homework, Project, Exam, Essay, and Quiz inherit common assignment information and functionality from the Assignment parent class.
Encapsulation: Assignment information is stored in private fields and accessed through getter and setter methods.
Polymorphism: The program stores different assignment subclasses in one List<Assignment>. 
This allows the application to manage different assignment types using the same list.
Abstraction: The parent Assignment class provides shared functionality so that subclasses do not need to repeat the same code.

8. Program Limitations
The current version of the Homework Tracker has several limitations:
Assignments are stored in memory and are not saved after the program closes.
Assignment names are used to identify assignments, so duplicate names may cause confusion.
The program does not support multiple courses or class schedules.
Users must manually select filters and sorting options.
The assignment display may become difficult to navigate when a large number of assignments are added.

9. Conclusion
The Homework Tracker provides a way for students to organize assignments and monitor their completion status. 
The project demonstrates Java programming concepts,object-oriented design, inheritance, ArrayLists, filtering, sorting, 
and JavaFX graphical user interface development. 

